<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdullah I of Jordan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1882–1951</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdullah I of Jordan, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, was a leader of the Arab Revolt and became emir of Transjordan under the British mandate in 1921. On 25 May 1946 he was proclaimed king of the independent kingdom, renamed Jordan in 1949. His Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> and East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the 1948 war, and he was assassinated in Jerusalem in 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdullah-i-of-jordan.jpg" alt="Abdullah I of Jordan" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cecil Beaton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cecil_Beaton_Photographs-_Political_and_Military_Personalities;_Abdullah,_King_of_Jordan;_Abdullah,_King_of_Jordan_CBM1666.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
